  "question": {
    "text": "Let $\\vec{\\alpha}, \\vec{\\beta}, \\vec{\\gamma}$ be the three unit vectors such that $\\vec{\\alpha} \\cdot \\vec{\\beta}=\\vec{\\alpha} \\cdot \\vec{\\gamma}=0$ and the angle between $\\vec{\\beta}$ and $\\vec{\\gamma}$\r\nis $30^{\\circ} .$ Then $\\vec{\\alpha}$ is"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "$2(\\vec{\\beta} \\times \\vec{\\gamma})$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$-2(\\vec{\\beta} \\times \\vec{y})$",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "$\\pm 2(\\vec{\\beta} \\times \\vec{\\gamma})$",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "$(\\vec{\\beta} \\times \\vec{y})$",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "C"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\vec{\\alpha}=\\lambda(\\vec{\\beta} \\times \\vec{\\gamma})=\\lambda\\left(|\\vec{\\beta}||\\vec{\\gamma}|\\left|\\sin 30^{\\circ}\\right|\\right.$\r\n$\\Rightarrow \\quad|\\vec{\\alpha}|=|\\lambda|\\left(|\\beta||\\vec{\\gamma}| \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\right)$\r\n$\\begin{array}{lc}\\Rightarrow & 1=|\\lambda| \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\Rightarrow & |\\lambda|=2 \\\\ \\rightarrow & \\lambda-\\pm 2\\end{array}$\r\n$\\therefore \\quad \\vec{\\alpha}=\\pm 2(\\vec{\\beta} \\times \\vec{\\gamma})$"
  }
}